counterfactuals.output:
A method for installing a protective tube mat over a bottom and an embankment of a landfill basin, the method comprising:

providing a flexible tube mat formed from a multi-ply geotextile fabric, the multi-ply geotextile fabric comprising at least two superposed, water-permeable geotextile plies joined to one another along a plurality of substantially parallel seams, the parallel seams defining between adjacent seams a plurality of elongated, substantially parallel fillable tubes;

positioning the flexible tube mat in an unfilled or partially filled condition over at least a portion of a bottom surface of the landfill basin and over at least a portion of an inclined embankment surface adjoining the bottom surface, such that the elongated fillable tubes extend in a predetermined orientation across the bottom surface and along or across the embankment surface;

anchoring or temporarily securing an upper edge region of the flexible tube mat relative to the embankment to prevent displacement of the mat during filling;

connecting at least one filling conduit to the fillable tubes of the tube mat; and

introducing into the fillable tubes an aqueous suspension comprising water and a solid particulate filling material, thereby hydraulically filling the tubes, wherein water from the aqueous suspension drains through the water-permeable multi-ply geotextile fabric while the solid particulate filling material is retained within the tubes, so that the tubes become weighted and expanded to form a protective, conforming covering over the bottom and embankment of the landfill basin.

In one embodiment, the parallel seams are formed by stitching, welding, bonding, or a combination thereof, and are spaced apart to define tubular chambers extending substantially continuously over the installed area. The aqueous suspension may comprise sand, soil, mineral aggregate, cementitious material, or another ballast-forming particulate material. Upon dewatering through the geotextile fabric, the retained solids form elongated ballast bodies within the tubes, thereby stabilizing the mat against uplift, sliding, erosion, and mechanical damage to an underlying sealing layer or liner of the landfill basin.
